1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,431
In Part 1, we used PESTEL to understand the big outside forces shaping Aalayam by Anamika.

2
00:00:06,351 --> 00:00:17,448
But that's only the beginning. Next, we needed to understand the competition: who else is selling, how much power customers and suppliers have, and how easy it is for new brands to join in.

3
00:00:17,368 --> 00:00:23,687
For that, we used Michael Porter's Five Forces: rivalry, new entrants, suppliers, buyers and substitutes.

4
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:25,848
We followed five steps.

5
00:00:25,768 --> 00:00:32,228
First, we revisited the business: Aalayam's products, its customers, and its place in Indian women's fashion.

6
00:00:32,148 --> 00:00:39,059
Second, we wrote research questions for each force, like: how easily can a shopper find a similar outfit somewhere else?

7
00:00:38,979 --> 00:00:41,911
Third, we collected data, online and in person.

8
00:00:41,831 --> 00:00:49,099
Fourth, we organised everything under the five forces, comparing competitors, prices, product types and customer feedback.

9
00:00:49,019 --> 00:00:53,821
And fifth, we analysed the pressure from each force, and turned it into recommendations.

10
00:00:54,401 --> 00:00:56,340
So, where did our data come from?

11
00:00:56,260 --> 00:01:05,733
Google Search gave us industry information and trends. On Myntra, AJIO and Amazon India, we compared competitor products, prices, ratings and reviews.

12
00:01:05,653 --> 00:01:16,938
Instagram showed how independent fashion brands market themselves, Google Trends showed what people search for, and Shopify and other marketplaces showed how easy it is to start selling online.

13
00:01:16,858 --> 00:01:26,158
We then checked this against our own primary research: customer interviews and surveys, supplier quotes and conversations, and a close look at competitor products.

14
00:01:26,078 --> 00:01:32,976
One more source we'd suggest for next time is IndiaMART, to compare fabric suppliers and manufacturers more widely.

15
00:01:33,556 --> 00:01:37,835
Force one: competitive rivalry, or how intense the competition is.

16
00:01:37,755 --> 00:01:44,386
We compared competitors' prices, product types, reviews and branding on Myntra, AJIO, Amazon and Instagram.

17
00:01:44,306 --> 00:01:47,412
Shoppers see many similar outfits side by side.

18
00:01:47,332 --> 00:01:53,190
So Aalayam should stand out through comfort, design and a recognisable brand, not on price alone.

19
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:55,932
Force two: the threat of new entrants.

20
00:01:55,852 --> 00:02:01,737
We looked at Shopify, marketplaces and Instagram to see how easy it is to start a clothing brand.

21
00:02:01,657 --> 00:02:06,471
Getting started online is relatively easy, so new competitors can appear quickly.

22
00:02:06,391 --> 00:02:11,644
Aalayam's best defence is loyalty: a consistent style, and customers who come back.

23
00:02:12,224 --> 00:02:14,453
Force three: the power of suppliers.

24
00:02:14,373 --> 00:02:21,739
Through supplier quotes and conversations, we looked at fabric costs, minimum order sizes, reliability and delivery times.

25
00:02:21,659 --> 00:02:23,975
Relying on a single supplier is a risk.

26
00:02:23,895 --> 00:02:29,593
Aalayam could compare costs, keep quality checks, and build relationships with more than one supplier.

27
00:02:30,173 --> 00:02:32,196
Force four: the power of buyers.

28
00:02:32,116 --> 00:02:38,747
Reviews, search trends, surveys and interviews showed us how customers compare prices and move between brands.

29
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:41,759
With so many choices, customers have real power.

30
00:02:41,679 --> 00:02:45,208
Good quality, transparent prices and trust can bring them back.

31
00:02:45,788 --> 00:02:54,412
Force five: the threat of substitutes, meaning other ways to meet the same need, like local boutiques, tailored outfits, or different styles altogether.

32
00:02:54,332 --> 00:02:57,742
We looked at listings, competitor sites and customer interviews.

33
00:02:57,662 --> 00:03:00,546
Customers always have other options to choose from.

34
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:05,882
Aalayam can stay the natural choice by focusing on comfort, practicality and good value.

35
00:03:06,462 --> 00:03:12,544
Here's how the findings turn into strategy. These are recommendations, not things Aalayam does today.

36
00:03:12,464 --> 00:03:20,894
Rivalry points to brand differentiation. New entrants point to loyalty and recognition. Supplier power points to a smarter sourcing plan.

37
00:03:20,814 --> 00:03:28,155
Buyer power points to a better customer experience and clearer value. And substitutes point to stronger product positioning.

38
00:03:28,075 --> 00:03:32,013
Together, they can help Aalayam build an advantage that's hard to copy.

39
00:03:32,593 --> 00:03:42,070
So that's the journey: understand the business, ask the questions, collect the data, analyse the five forces, find the insights, and turn them into recommendations.

40
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:55,632
Through PESTEL, we understood the outside environment. Through Porter's Five Forces, we understood the competition. Together, they show where Aalayam operates, what challenges it faces, and how it can build a position that's truly its own.
